A||N||23||stimpy||Where do you find this stuff?||||1||
Z||000000||stimpy||01-17-2000||11:46 PM||||<BR>I have been visiting this forum for about a year now occaisionally, having recently become a member of this prestigidous group. While runnig through old threads, it seems like anyone who paid more then $17 and some pocket lint for their (insert any model other than 88 GT here) got robbed.  And then they were rebuilt using only the best parts that were obtained trading Pokemon cards.  I am beginning to feel like a bit of an eediot for the bucks shelled out on my 86 GT. (Not t mention the skeptical sideways glances from the soon-to-be Mrs. Stimpy)  Has anyone else actually wound up with a $6000 daily driver, or did they really see me coming? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Dave85||01-17-2000||11:58 PM||||Don't feel to bad, a guy that I know paid $8,900 for his '87 GT about a year ago.  I just missed an '87 GT with only 75,000 miles in perfect shape for $1,400. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||01-18-2000||12:08 AM||||Perfect example.  I just can't help thinking that there is an element of "fish story" when it comes to low mile GT astbacks for under $2g.  I thought I was getting a good deal when I originally bought my car w 121k for $2500. I guess having the disposable cash around when the opportunity arises and having the ability to strike while the iron is hot have a lot to do withit, but it makes me wonder if there is some benevolent Fiero Fairy bequething good girls and boys with mnt low mile 88 GTs and Formulas for $2000  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||01-18-2000||12:16 AM||||After a year of looking for an 88 I found a silver formula for $7000US.  I passed.  Another year went by and I found a black gt for $7000US.  I took it even though it wasn't as nice as the formula, the formula was no longer for sale.  Price depends on market.  But it does hurt to see all those give away prices.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||01-18-2000||12:47 AM||||Ok the dream car find:  Its a white 88GT 30,000 on the dial, this car is in show room condition all stock, Price $3,000.<P>Is this a deal??    Can it happen?<P>Ok the car belongs to a friend of a friends mother-in-laws brothers girl friend, shes in the army over seas, he needs money to buy them a house, she gives him the of to sell the car(quick) he dosen't know anything about fieros or cars. You get the deal because you are that friend.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>THIS IS NOT REAL&gt;&gt;&gt; AGAIN THIS IS NOT REAL!!!||||reg||6
Z||000005||mutfish||01-18-2000||06:35 AM||||I'm not ashamed to tell anyone that I paid 5 k for my 88 GT. Although it wasn't "perfect" when I bought it, it does have only 64,000+ original miles and a lot of options. Over the last year or so I put close to another 1k in it,and just painted it.$7000.00 or so to have a Fiero that looks as good as any NEW car out there? I consider that a deal.....<p>[This message has been edited by mutfish (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||01-18-2000||06:43 AM||||I am like fishmut, I paid $12,900 for my fiero *485 miles*. No regrets, but I do think that my next one will be one that I can modify and drive more frequently.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||01-18-2000||08:57 AM||||I think that one of two things has to happen to catch a real deal on a Fiero.<P>1.  Find a car that someone thinks has a very serious problem and is just plain tired of working on.  <P>2.  Find someone who wants (or needs) cash more than he/she wants to break even on the car.  <P>It seems to be the case with the Fastbacks that they are hard to find for under 3k sometimes.  Why?  Their owners are just like us.  They put alot of hard work and pride into keeping the car on the road and looking good.  And they may have a bit of an inflated opinion on what the car is worth.  Also, you may not be willing to pay $3500-$4000 for a Fiero, but the next guy who comes along to look at that car may have $4k burning a hole in his pocket.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||01-18-2000||09:20 AM||||There's nothing wrong with shelling out a few bucks for a Fiero - sometimes you get what you pay for!<P>In late 2000, my wife and I plan to buy our first home.  Then I'm going Fiero shopping with a fairly big budget.  I want a nice, tight, beautiful, never-wrecked, well-maintained car and I'm willing to pay for it.||||reg||6
Z||000009||deceler8||01-18-2000||09:57 AM||||While I haven't seen many buys like GT's for $500, there are a lot of 4 cylinder cars out there for great prices. I paid $500 for my '86 2m4, and while it needed some work (only cosmetic), many people have commented on that "nice little sports car" I drive. <P>No one believes it only cost $500, and many are surprised it's as old as it is.<P>If you limit your search to GT's and Formulas in the $500 price range, you may be looking for a long time, while a 2m4 may be right under your nose for that price. And the 2m4 is a very competent car, in my opinion. Still has the head turning looks...you may give up a little performance, but you gain better fuel economy and easier maintenance (IMO).<P>||||reg||10
Z||000010||HF6582||01-18-2000||10:33 AM||||I bought my 86GT for $400. the seller lost interest in the car and wanted to get rid of it. It does need painted and the drivers seat is ripped, but for that price I can fix it. Right now I am working on a trans change over (going from auto to stick). That swap is almost finished. I also have a file full of of the service records from the orignal onwer. It had a new motor put in at 95k  and it now has 125k.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||01-18-2000||11:02 AM||||Well I've got a few bucks in my yellow(by the end of this week I hope) Formula. I was going to by an new car to replace my 92 AWD Laser and after checking prices $20K+ for something I would want I decided that I could build up a Fiero with a SC3800 for much less than that. The car is almost totally new in every respect very reliable and trouble free. The only thing left is the headliner (starting to get a few wrinkles). The end result is that I have a basicly brand new car that will out-perform just about anything on the road, insurance is low, looks good etc. and I have just a little over half of what a new car would have cost. Plus I still have my Laser(good for snow). BTW this is the first "used car" I have owned in 30 yrs(last one was a 67 XKE) Is it worth it? I think so.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||01-18-2000||11:07 AM||||Another tale: I did buy a &lt;20K mi 88 GT for $3000 but had to put $1000 more into it to be mint. So is $4k too much  for a perfect 20Kmi 88GT? You make your decision I made mine. <BR>The Formula is my daily driver and the GT is my son's daily car.<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||01-18-2000||12:07 PM||||For all of you that have many $ invested in very nice Fieros and are getting cut rate prices on insurance just remember that if the car gets totaled or stolen the insurance won't give more that a couple thousand for it unless you have negotiated better coverage.<P>I think about that more and more as I continue to pour money into upgrading mine.<P>Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phil||01-18-2000||12:11 PM||||It's been done||||reg||1
Z||000015||DRH||01-18-2000||12:16 PM||||stimpy, a lot of people win a decent amount of money in one of our many lotteries in this country every single day. Does that mean if you go buy a ticket you will win?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>There is a big difference between a "fair" price and a great deal someone lucked into. $2500 for a decent 86 GT sounds fair. How did you spend the other $3500? If you wound up with a reliable 14 year old car for $6000 I don't think you got cheated... You just didn't win the lotto.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||mshill||01-18-2000||03:45 PM||||Much of it depends on what the buyer wants in a Fiero.  I bought my 86SE for $500 and will have over $2500 in it (nearly perfect) when I am done with it.  The items I had to fix are typically cheap to have fixed by a shop.  My 86GT is an asthetic nightmare but very mechanically sound.  I got it for $500, for a kit car donor.<P>If you want a low miles perfect car, then you are going to pay for it.  If you want a project, that's where you find the sub $1000 deals out there.  In my area the V6 cars don't last long on the market, regardless of<BR>condition.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||01-18-2000||05:00 PM||||I'm in the middle of paying $500 for an '85 GT with a bad motor (supposedly).  Aftermarket wheels, custom paint, etc.  The seller is a college student.  Those of us who have been there know that sometimes a little bit of pocket change is well worth selling a car cheap.  I've found a donor car for the motor cheap too.  (One of you forum members.  I'll leave his name blank for the time being.)  Not counting the interior, I shouldn't have more than about $800 in a good looking, good running GT.  It's not a fastback, but then again, I prefer notchbacks, so I think my deal is better than you might think it is.  (That made sense in my mind, but you know how my mind is!)||||reg||1
Z||000018||fierosound||01-18-2000||06:33 PM||||There seems to be a W-I-D-E price range on Fieros, everything from $500 to $9000 depending on model/year.  I figured I got a good deal when I bought my 1987 Fiero GT for CDN$9750 with 27,000kms (US$7000 / 16,875 miles)in 1990!!  The way I looked at it I was getting a practically new car for under $10,000.  With it still looking nearly new in/out I supposed it would still be worth something if I were to sell it - not that I ever plan to.... <P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierosound</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000019||stimpy||01-18-2000||07:18 PM||||Thanks for asking DRH. My first year of Fiero ownership was pretty uneventful on the money part. My only real expenses were new EGR tube, new battery and new rear tires.  Last June brought about the need to open the floodgates, cashflow wise.  On a round trip between Central CA and LA to pick up my fathers 3.8sc 86 GT from Chris West @ West Coast Fieros (unsolcited plug!) my bottom end turned into shrapnel at 85 mph about 15 miles from home.  The car sat for several months and I just got it out of the shop. My rebuild cost included junkyard donor engine ($200) Rebuild of junkyard motor with various ad sundry new parts.. oil pan, harmonic balancer, water pump, Accel Super coil, 8mm Accel Spiral wound wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, and a really cool refinishing of intake and valve covers in yellow powdercoat to match my future paint job.($2700 total)<BR>Yes, the paint needs rspraying, the sail panel windows are spiderwebbed, and the Leather seats have seen better days.  This car will probably never be truely "done" and will only cost me more and more.  But it's value to me is not the $$$ I have sunk into it but the sure thrll of taking onramps at 70 mph and knowing that I am not yet at the limit.  Who can put a price tag on that?  ||||reg||1
Z||000020||DRH||01-18-2000||07:49 PM||||Stimpy, <BR>I hear you! The next time the future Mrs Stimpy gives you one of those looks... take her new car shopping and show her how much payments (not to mention insurance) would be on anything that would hit that onramp at 70 the same way your car does.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>$2700 is not a bad price for what you had done. <P>BTW, where's Ren???<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||stimpy||01-18-2000||08:05 PM||||Ren is my 86 GT, gold with a luggage rack. Weird enough for you?||||reg||1
Z||000022||HotFieroGT||01-18-2000||10:43 PM||||I bought my red 88GT auto. for $6200.00 back in 1993, it was a nice car with 47,000 miles on it.  Came across a black 88GT w 5/speed with 89,000 miles on it a couple of months ago and picked it up for $1000.00.  It needs a little work but is a steal for $1000.00, sometimes you make out like a bandit and other times........   ||||reg||6
Z||000023||stsmithgt||01-19-2000||05:32 PM||||I believe I got a good deal when I bought my white w/grey int. 86 GT with pw,pl,a/c,cc,rear defrost,sunroof,everything works @ 74,000 miles for 2100.00. since then for three years I sunk 1000.00 dollars per year average into it, but some of that was for other rims and a cool steering wheel.  I have 109,000 miles on it now.  I still have the old steering wheel.  It needs to be re skinned, but the horn button is fine that says GT.  I sold my GT rims for 120.00 I'm not sure if I gave him a good deal or not.  anybody want an old Fiero GT steering wheel?||||reg||10
